    "title": "Texas Roots: The Untold Story of Fajitas - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Dominique ,\n12 hours ago\nFajitas, a quintessential dish in Tex-Mex cuisine, trace their origins to the Texas ranchlands and the traditions of Mexican cowboys (vaqueros).\nThe preparation of this dish emerged in the 1930s among Mexican and Tejano ranch hands along the Rio Grande, particularly in South and West Texas.\nDownload the KZBT-KGEE Mobile App Now The Birth of the Fajita The ranch hands and laborers in South Texas were frequently compensated with less desirable cuts of beef , such as skirt steak (known as “falda” in Spanish), which they skillfully prepared over open flames, wrapped in tortillas, and seasoned.\nThomas Park via Unsplash The term “fajita” originates from the Spanish word “faja,” which translates to “belt” or “strip,” reflecting the method of cutting the skirt steak.\nAlthough the word “fajita” wasn’t documented until 1971, the preparation of this dish was already commonplace.\nCommercialization of Fajitas Fajitas were traditionally an essential part of ranch life and family gatherings, serving as a staple during communal meals.\nHowever, their emergence into the realm of commercial cuisine began in the late 1960s.\nIn 1969, a series of pivotal events contributed to the popularization of fajitas, allowing them to transition from home kitchens and backyard barbecues to restaurants and public fairs.\nThis shift marked a significant turning point, as fajitas became a beloved dish featured on menus across the country, showcasing their vibrant flavors and diverse ingredients to a wider audience.\nRead More: No Beans, Big Flavor: The Best Chili in Texas\nCommon Misconception of Fajitas Origins Fajitas are a staple of Tex-Mex. However, one common misconception was that the fajita was created in Mexico.\nDespite where it was born, one thing is definitely for certain: Tex-Mex wouldn’t be the same without it. Its popularity has spread far beyond the borders of Texas and is enjoyed by countless people throughout the world.\nFall-Themed Food and Products Invade Store Shelves Gallery Credit: Dominique Garcia Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Pie Pop-Tarts Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Muffin Mix Dominique Garcia Cinnamon Pumpkin Pie Soap Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Hand Soap Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Coffee Creamer Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Under Eye Patches Dominique Garcia Sweet Pumpkin Candles Dominique Garcia Cinnamon Plug In Scents Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Cookies Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Roll Fabreze Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Milano Cookies Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Scones Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Walnut Cake Dominique Garcia Halloween-Themed Oreos Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Pie Ice Cream Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Pretzels Dominique Garcia Pumpkin Spice Baking Mix The 10 Most Popular Thanksgiving Foods in Texas Thanksgiving will be here soon. Here are the most popular Thanksgiving foods in the Lone Star State. Gallery Credit: Billy Jenkins Canva 10. Pumpkin Pie A Thanksgiving classic, often sharing the dessert table with pecan pie. Canva 9. Gravy Gravy is a must for dressing and mashed potatoes. Canva 8. Cranberry Sauce Served alongside turkey, sometimes with a jalapeño twist for a Texan flair. Canva 7. Macaroni and Cheese Creamy and cheesy, this side dish is popular at Texas Thanksgiving feasts. Canva 6. Tamales A Tex-Mex addition to many Thanksgiving tables, especially in South Texas. Canva 5. Pecan Pie A favorite dessert in Texas, often made with locally sourced pecans. 4. Sweet Potato Casserole Topped with marshmallows or pecans for a sweet, Southern touch. Canva 3. Green Bean Casserole A classic side dish with green beans, cream of mushroom soup, and crispy fried onions. Canva 2. Cornbread Dressing A Southern staple, often flavored with sage or sausage, replacing bread-based stuffing. Canva 1. Smoked Turkey Instead of the classic roasted turkey, many Texans prefer their turkey smoked or barbecued for extra flavor. Try getting a Greenberg turkey!",
